Free oscillations receive no continuing supply of mechanical energy and gradually decay when damping is present;
ideal undamped free oscillations can retain their amplitude, so "no decay" alone cannot distinguish free oscillations from self-oscillations.
The energy and amplitude of free oscillations are determined by the initial conditions. This differs from self-oscillations, which receive a continuing energy supply and approach a stable operating state.

